# What is translated of this program's messages, in French.
#
# Keyed by the English wording, the same as the German file beside it.
# All of the program's texts stand here since 12.9.2026 -- the captions
# the window carries and the report that runs beside them, Resolve
# included. Until then it was the captions alone, 336 of 1467. Every
# text that is not in this file appears in English: T() gives back the
# key it was handed when it finds no entry, so what is missing reads
# rather than breaks.
#
# These translations were written by Claude, an Anthropic model, out of
# what it knows. No translation service was asked, no French speaker
# has read them, and nobody has reviewed a single line. Treat every
# entry as a first draft. Correcting one costs nothing but this file:
# delete an entry and that text is English again.
#
# The 1131 texts and 31 counted things added on 12.9.2026 were written
# by one model and read back by two others. They checked 231 entries by
# hand and found eight places where the meaning had slipped -- a gender
# pointing at the wrong noun, a false friend ("propre" for "of your
# own", which nachgestellt means clean), a unit the program writes in
# English, a verb in the plural where the count can be nought. Six more
# came from searching for the same pattern elsewhere. All fourteen were
# repaired before this file was written.
#
# Measured that way, 4.2 and 2.7 slips per hundred entries checked --
# against 8.8 for the Spanish run, which was made without the list of
# traps that grew out of it.
#
# One place could not be repaired here and is not a fault of this file:
# the program builds a plural by hanging an "s" on a translated word,
# which gives "fichier vidéos" where French wants "fichiers vidéo".
# The head carries the count in French, and no catalogue can reach it.
#
# One entry per message: msgid is the English wording the
# program is written in, msgstr what is said instead. An
# entry left out appears in English.
#
# A counted thing is the exception to that one entry. It carries
# msgid_plural and one msgstr[i] per wording this language has, in
# place of the single msgstr, and the Plural-Forms line in the
# header below says how many that is and which count wants which.
# Two here, and nought goes with the singular in French:
# [0] where the count is 0 or 1, [1] from two upward.
#
# The wordings are read as a list, so a form left empty costs that
# form and not the entry. Every form carries the same %s the
# English wording does: the program applies the count to whatever
# comes back, and a form without it stops the run on a number
# nobody tried.
#
# The English plural keeps its own ordinary entry beside such a
# group. It is not spare: it is what a run falls back on where the
# rule cannot be read at all. The English singular has no entry of
# its own -- that would be the same msgid twice.
